WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.140
Good morning John.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.140
Today I’m going to teach you how

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.520
to do one of the most important
things you can do to help

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.759
mitigate climate change.

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:07.810
It’s something anyone can do,

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.300
and if you watch this video you
will be better at it than almost

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.750
everyone on Earth.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.350
But first,
a little background.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:16.350
Every 6 or

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.380
7 years, the UN puts out a report
about climate change: where we

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.320
are, what it looks like, how we’re
doing, and one of those came out

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:23.320
this week.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.460
And the result was a
whole lot of headlines that do

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.910
not look good.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.339
And yeah,
things aren’t good.

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:32.579
But also I was really glad to
have come across this tweet:

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:35.120
“As a climate scientist,
I’d like you to know: I don’t

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.120
have hope.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.079
I have something better:
certainty.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.079
We know exactly

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:40.260
what’s causing climate change.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.519
We can absolutely avoid the worst

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:45.239
and build a better world in the process.

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:46.629
And that is certainly nice.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.449
We know what is causing
this problem.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.199
We know how to solve it.

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:53.399
And indeed, in a way we are.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:55.219
Like renewable energy is skyrocketing

00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:58.390
and yeah solar is producing just 1% of
the world’s energy right now

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.749
but it’s increasing faster every year.

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:02.550
Per capita CO2 emissions in the US

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.170
are lower than they were in 1918.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.490
Now that was some very cherry-picked data.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:08.990
Things are not good.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.670
We are running
an experiment on our life support

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.440
system and we know that the result
is going to be bad we just don’t

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.440
know how bad.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.909
The IPCC report is clear,
we are not headed in a good direction.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:22.450
To avoid the worst consequences
we are going to have to keep global

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.310
increase in temperature below 2 degrees
Celsius or a little less than 4 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.700
Currently we are not on target to
do that even if we keep to all the

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:31.840
agreements we have.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.159
We need to
quickly stop putting greenhouse

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:38.630
gases into the atmosphere and
also quickly start taking them

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:39.630
back out.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.520
Now the two most common
questions I am asked about climate change

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.810
are 1.) is there actually any hope here?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:45.810
And 2.)

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:46.810
what should I do?

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.329
Now as for the first question, yes.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:50.009
Well it depends on what you mean,

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.000
is there hope that we will experience
no negative impacts from climate change?

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.000
No.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.000
No there’s not.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.130
We’re already
experiencing them.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.130
So climate change

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.130
is going to make life worse.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.870
But we don’t
know how much worse, and if you’re

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.969
watching this video the chances
are it’s going to be much worse for

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:05.079
other people than it is for you.

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:06.350
Global warming, like almost every

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.080
issue, is a justice issue.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.229
It will have
the biggest impacts on the people

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.360
who are least responsible for it.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.780
People who are relatively affluent

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.300
and live in relatively affluent
places will be mostly, though not all, ok.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.880
Like not great, but okay.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.320
But the less you have, the harder

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.090
it is to deal with the destabilizations
that come from a changing climate.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:27.129
And as for the second, like a lot.

00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:28.480
You
should be doing a lot.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.480
But you also

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.420
have other stuff to do, like you
have to take care of your friends

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.620
and family, you have to go school,
you gotta pay your loans, you gotta

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.350
do your work, you gotta goof off
sometimes.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.900
But in between all of that,

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.010
yes, climate change is the most
important and difficult issue humanity faces.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:47.040
And so, there are things you can do
both to show the world this is a crisis,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.360
and to mitigate your impact.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.351
Eat less or no meat, especially

00:02:50.351 --> 00:02:54.640
not beef, never buy a gas powered
car ever again if you can avoid it,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.159
especially not a new one.

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:57.890
Prefer smaller homes, don’t buy

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:01.270
gas stoves, live closer to where
you work, but also, one of the most

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:05.069
important things anyone can do,
and anyone can actually do this

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:09.120
one, is to be informed on the
different policies and laws that

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.470
governments create to try and
solve these problems.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.470
Because we

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.460
can’t do this without an
informed public, and policies need

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.550
advocates, so they need people
who understand it, so we can have

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:22.370
these conversations in ways that
make sense, and unfortunately it’s

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.780
pretty complicated so it isn’t
immediately obvious how any of

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:26.780
this works.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.700
But if you just watch
this video you are going to be more

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.300
informed than 99% of the people in
your country on how this stuff works.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:35.300
This one video.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.980
Now you can go
deeper than that and I encourage you to,

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.660
but this is a good place to start.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.290
But before we do that, a couple points

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.290
of clarification.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.890
One, I’m going to be
saying carbon a lot.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:43.890
And when I say

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.099
carbon, I mean carbon dioxide which
is the chief heat-trapping gas that

00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:48.530
we emit into the atmosphere.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.540
But I don’t just mean carbon dioxide,

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:55.220
I also mean the equivalent amounts
of other gases that also trap heat,

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.690
methane being the primary one.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.360
Methane actually traps 84x the heat

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.110
of carbon dioxide, we just release
a lot less of it thankfully.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.110
So we have

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.659
equivalency measures, so 1 ton of
methane released will be equivalent

00:04:07.659 --> 00:04:10.170
to 84 tons of CO2.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.950
This way we
end up talking about all greenhouse

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.769
gases in the frame of carbon dioxide
which is why people often just say carbon.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:19.600
Now second, we’re used to thinking of
global warming as a problem of, like,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.510
our transportation, maybe our homes,
maybe agriculture.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:24.510
Because those

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:25.950
are the things we have the most direct
contact with.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.260
But this is a big, mixed up

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.260
jumble of a problem.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.860
You can pause the
video if you want to get a low down

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.420
about where all the carbon is coming
from, this is a very useful chart from

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.300
Dr. Anna Ritchie at Our World in Data.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:36.300
Alright, here we are.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.300
We have actually arrived.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.640
We’re going to talk about what strategies
governments use and can use to

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.680
rapidly decrease their emissions
of greenhouse gases.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.680
And there are

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.350
big categories of these, there’s carbon
pricing, and then there’s everything else.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.580
Carbon pricing looks at the problem
with a pretty clear vision, if releasing

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.970
carbon dioxide is free, people don’t
have any reason not to do it.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:57.970
But it’s

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:01.630
actually not free, it’s going to cost a
tremendous amount of money to deal

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.200
with the consequences of the emission of that
carbon.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.560
But that money isn’t being paid

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.250
by the people producing the emissions
or the people consuming the energy,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:13.970
the gas, the steel, the concrete that
create the emissions, those costs

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.920
will be born by people in the future
and they will be wildfires and

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.610
droughts and famines and wars and
refugees and sea level rise.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:21.610
All of those

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.160
are costs that are not being priced in.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.020
So carbon pricing just includes those

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.900
costs, and there are 3 main ways to do that.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.140
The first is just pricing carbon,

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.340
and this actually weirdly has had some
pretty bipartisan support.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.340
People further

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.230
on the right or the left don’t like this,
but in the center where market based

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:43.110
solutions feel really good, people like this.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.630
The basic idea is that whenever a

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:48.790
fossil fuel or another source of emissions
enters the economy, whether it’s at the

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:52.370
moment it’s taken out of the ground or
at the moment of entry when we import it,

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:53.370
you tax it.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:56.990
You say, “this will soon be burned,
and that will produce a ton of carbon

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:01.450
and a ton of carbon is going to cost the
world $50 to deal with in the future,

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:04.990
so we’re going to make this thing
cost $50 extra”.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:05.990
This is by far the

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:08.260
simplest thing I’m going to talk about
today to actually implement.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:11.110
It’s easy to regulate, you don’t have
to worry about different industries

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:15.000
lobbying to get better deals, and
everyone pays the same flat rate

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.730
and this significantly increases the
cost of fossil fuels, thus encouraging

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:20.930
investment and innovation in other
options.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.020
Now there’s a sub category

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.180
of this idea, which is what do you then
do with all of this money?

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.180
Any carbon

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.980
price that is anywhere near the actual
long term cost of releasing the carbon will

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.900
at first generate a lot of cash.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.450
And there are
two basic ideas: the first is to use it to

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.460
the work of decarbonizing our economy.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.400
Invest in new research, mass transit, solar

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.160
power plants, job training for people
who used to work in the fossil industry.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.610
But the second idea confronts the
biggest problem with the carbon tax,

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.250
which is that it is a tax.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:51.300
Pricing
carbon increases the costs of

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.640
heating a home, of commuting to
work, of flying to see your family.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.220
And nobody likes that.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.150
So instead
of giving the money to governments

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.950
to invest in decarbonizing the economy,
the current more favored and more

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:08.030
palatable plan, is just to take the money
that we raise and give it to people.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:11.990
You divide the pot by exactly the
number of people who live in the country,

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:14.690
and you give everyone the exact
same check.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:15.690
This is called the carbon

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:16.870
fee and dividend.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.820
Now the money that
comes out of this isn’t enough to be

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.580
like a universal basic income kind of thing,
but it is enough that it covers the increase

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:26.230
costs of the heating and the driving for
most people.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:27.620
Not for the wealthiest people,

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:32.220
but mostly for people who are on the lower
end of the income spectrum, their extra

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:33.220
costs will be covered.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.701
But that doesn’t
mean that the system is totally fair, people

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:39.010
in rural areas use a lot more energy than
people in the cities.

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.010
But everyone is

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:42.520
going to get the same sized check,
and I think that lack of fairness will

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.190
probably cause some
tension.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:45.190
Just guessing.

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:47.570
Now another problem with the
carbon tax is that it treats everything

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.280
the same, which is what a lot of
people like about it, but some

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:54.170
industries are way easier to decarbonize
than others.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:55.660
And some are far more

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.520
hot politically like people care a lot
more about gas prices than they do

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.950
about steel prices.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.780
So what if you
could create a plan that found the

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.770
cheapest ways to decarbonize and
prioritized them first so you have roughly

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:12.080
the same amount of carbon decreasing
but you give some more time to the

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.510
industries where it’s more difficult to
decarbonize.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:15.510
That sounds like something

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.300
that a market would be good at.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.370
And
thus we have, cap and trade.

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:23.090
Again, very basically, there’s a cap
that’s the total amount of emissions

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:27.570
that the government’s going to allow, and
then they distribute or sell permits to

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.230
different polluters.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:32.250
And then those polluters,
if they don’t use up all their permits,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.250
can sell

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:36.620
or trade on an open market
their permits for pollution.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:39.740
As the caps get lower with time, the
permits become more scarce and

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:43.750
more expensive, thus decarbonizing
becomes more worth it.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:44.750
This worked

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.410
extremely well in decreasing the
amount of sulfur dioxide released

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.420
by coal plants in the 1980s.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:50.930
Sulfur dioxide reacts with water in the

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.560
atmosphere to create sulfuric acid,
was making the rain literally acidic,

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.450
some pieces of statues washed away
and forests died in droves, and we

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:01.860
solved that problem with a cap and trade
system on sulfur emissions.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:02.860
This creates a

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:06.180
market that can act like a market and the
people who can most inexpensively decrease

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.530
their emissions do it first, and then over
time as the cap gets lower and lower and lower,

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:11.570
everybody has to join.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.440
But cap and trade
of course has it’s issues, certain pollutants

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:19.220
often start out with free polluter credits,
often times they can offset their pollution

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.850
super inexpensively with unproven credits,
and it’s very vulnerable to lobbying by

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:24.850
big

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:25.850
emitters.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:28.250
They can convince legislators that
they need to be carved out, or given more

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:31.950
permits, so that companies can afford
more lobbyists get better deals.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.900
Cap and trade does work, it’s being
used all over the world, it’s just more

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.670
complicated than it initially
sounds.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:37.670
As all of this is.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:41.540
Hank from the future, I forgot to say
that cap and trade also generates revenue,

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.070
so you have the same questions
about whether to spend it on

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:48.160
decarbonization or just distribute it
as a dividend or lower taxes or some

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.290
mix of those things.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.110
But both of those
systems we just talked about,

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.020
they intentionally increase the price
of carbon, that’s what we’ve been

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.020
talking about right?

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.020
It’s carbon pricing.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:57.180
We have to increase the price in order

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.410
to incentivize alternatives.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.040
But people
do not like it when their government is like,

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.980
“hey your prices are going to go up.”

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:03.980
Especially when they’re going to go up

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.910
for everybody and that’s going to
affect some people a lot more than others.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.780
It’s hard, though not impossible to get
people on board with this stuff.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.610
There are carbon taxes and cap and trade
schemes all over the world right now.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:18.140
But this does lead us to our final and
most futuristic option for pricing carbon.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:22.460
Instead of charging people for
releasing carbon, you pay them to stop

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:25.240
it from being released or to capture
it again.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.240
Kim Stanley Robinson the

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.140
science fiction writer calls this
“carbon quantitative easing” quantitative

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:32.800
easing being the weird economics
term for just dumping lots of money

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:33.800
into an economy.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.220
Basically this just
switches the carbon pricing on its head.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:39.110
Instead of saying “hey we’re going to
charge you for emitting,” we’re going

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.110
to

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.110
pay you for not emitting.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:42.110
And it makes
sense because there are two ways to think

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:45.840
about the costs of emitting carbon, one is
to say this is going to cost a lot of money

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.700
if we emit it, the other is to say this is
going to save a lot of money if we prevent

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:50.700
it from being emitted.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.630
So the ideas is,
as long as the US federal reserve is printing

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:57.700
money hand over fist, might as well tie
some positive result to that.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:58.700
Whoever can

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:02.070
prove that they prevented CO2 from
entering the atmosphere or permanently

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.830
stored it from the atmosphere just gets paid.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:06.830
They could potentially even get

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:11.850
paid with like a global carbon coin, a
currency that every country uses that’s

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.720
tied to the value of removal
of CO2 from the air.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:15.810
There are problems with this.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.300
Of course as well, there’s a large

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:21.470
bureaucracy necessary for counting these
things and preventing fraud.

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:22.470
The fossil

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:26.610
fuel companies would have to be paid,
given lots of money to keep their

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:29.660
carbon locked up and that feels a
little unfair, and then of course there’s

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.270
the tremendous cost.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:33.480
The taxes
generate revenue, this is

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:34.510
the opposite of that.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:37.330
Now frustratingly,
all carbon pricing strategies are looking

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:40.800
less and less likely in my country
the US right now.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:41.800
In part, because of

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:43.660
the reason many people like them.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.040
They’re simple economic strategies that

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.700
just price the carbon and let the market
figure everything else.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:48.700
But that also

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:52.220
means that there’s a tremendous
lack of control over what’s actually happening.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:56.030
And that means that maybe the
costs get pushed off onto marginalized

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:59.350
communities and that the benefits of
these new economic systems,

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:02.330
they’re going to go to the people
who have the access and the money

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:05.029
and the connections to build the
companies that will do all the work.

00:12:05.029 --> 00:12:07.440
Now does it matter who gets
rich if we get the carbon out of the

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.251
atmosphere, well, this is a thing that
people disagree about, especially the

00:12:11.251 --> 00:12:12.251
people on the left.

00:12:12.251 --> 00:12:15.040
And at the moment
in my country, it doesn’t matter what

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:18.160
people on the right think because they’re
not going to vote for any of this stuff.

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.150
So if you can’t get all the democrats to
vote for a carbon tax, you’re not going

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:22.150
to

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:23.150
get a carbon tax.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:25.120
Which is why in the
US we are currently planning to do

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.940
the other thing, not carbon pricing,
but just everything else.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:33.230
Passing tons of different laws and
policies and regulations that do

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.980
tons of different things.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:36.620
This is honestly
probably the more progressive way

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.320
to do things, to pay more attention
and control things more tightly,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.460
whereas the more conservative thing
to do would be, let’s create a market,

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:46.279
let everything handle itself, get the big
government out of it.

00:12:46.279 --> 00:12:47.279
And this

00:12:47.279 --> 00:12:51.580
hodgepodge of tons of new ideas,
they fall largely into 3 different groups.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:53.800
First, we have standards.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:55.870
Standards
are rules that affect what industries can

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:56.870
do.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:00.260
So cars have to be more efficient or
they have to be electric, power companies

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:03.510
have to decommission coal plants and
build wind and solar.

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:04.510
Instead of saying,

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:07.279
“hey it’s going to be a lot more expensive
to build fossil fuels in the future,”

00:13:07.279 --> 00:13:11.710
they just say “hey, by the year 2050
you can not be burning fossil fuels

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:12.710
anymore.”

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:13.810
The second is investment.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:15.880
The government looks for early,

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:19.500
mid and late stage opportunities to
help new technologies come to market.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:24.130
They spend capital on mass transit and
buy electric cars and buses and build

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:25.550
charging infrastructure.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.480
They help people
make their homes more energy efficient.

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:31.220
And the third and final leg of this stool
is

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.460
justice.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:33.810
Help people who are most affected

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:38.150
by decarbonization find fulfilling work,
spend money on the communities

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:41.660
who have been most negatively
impacted by existing polluting infrastructure.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:44.610
We’re going to be spending a ton
of money to solve this problem,

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:49.029
the people who benefit shouldn’t
just be the ones who created the problem.

00:13:49.029 --> 00:13:51.960
You sometimes see this slate of
the climate agenda referred to as

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:55.080
“SIJ” Standards Investment and
Justice.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.370
Now this falls short of a full

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:59.470
Green New Deal type framework
which would add even more on the

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:03.670
justice end with policies like Medicare
for all and a job guarantee.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:04.670
But it is a

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:09.060
very, very big plan that has not been
finalized much less passed.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.060
President Biden

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:13.940
has said though that he will not sign his
1.2 billion dollar infrastructure package

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:17.400
unless there is also a climate plan
on his desk.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.200
So like, here’s hoping.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:22.890
Now an important thing here, you
do not have to do just one of these things.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.870
You can have a carbon tax, and a
cap and trade system, and pay people

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:28.760
to remove carbon from the atmosphere,
and have standards investment and

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:29.930
justice regulations.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.020
And these plans
exist all over the place.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:33.020
China, Canada and

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:36.200
the whole European Union have a cap and
trade system, Japan, Turkey, Thailand, Indonesia

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:40.610
and a bunch of other places have
carbon taxes, and pretty much everywhere

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.180
has some amount of broad climate
regulation.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:44.180
And which of these things am

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.970
I most in favor of?

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:46.970
All of them.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:48.991
I want to
do anything we can get done.

00:14:48.991 --> 00:14:53.630
But it is super helpful to understand
the basics of how all of these things work.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:57.570
Especially because in this whole video
which has not been short or simple, I have

00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:00.160
only talked about what individual countries
do.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.620
But of course this can not be done by one

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:03.620
country.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:06.840
The conversation about how to
create a multi national set of enforceable

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:12.660
standards on carbon pricing or just targets
to achieve generally is an order of magnitude

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.790
more complicated even than any of this.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:17.150
But if you watched this video and you

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:21.440
understood it, you now have joined a
pretty exclusive club.

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:22.440
You are one of the

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:27.290
people who understands the basics of
how governments try and decrease their

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.180
greenhouse gas emissions through laws
and regulations.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:31.600
And that is amazing,

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:32.880
but you can go further.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.350
And I encourage
you to go further.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:35.440
I’d suggest a podcast

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.180
called Pricing Nature to understand the
complexities of carbon pricing and

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.410
international agreements.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.910
You should also
check out David Roberts’ newsletter at Volts.wtf

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:47.290
and if you want to get very wonky in a
science fiction framework, try out the Ministry

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:49.270
of the Future by Kim Stanley Robinson.

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:50.830
Also I have to say thank you so much to

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:55.220
the people at Our World In Data who
provide a tremendous service to our entire

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.600
world with a pretty tiny team.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.330
Their site and
all their information on climate change is

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:04.770
extremely illuminating to peruse and I
encourage you to go spend time there,

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.589
I’ve given them a 1,000 donation as a
thank you because of how much it helped

00:16:08.589 --> 00:16:09.730
me make this video.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:11.600
And also because you
cannot solve the problem

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:17.440
if you don’t understand it.

